The servicing app's origination income engine (spec §22.3, Fannie Mae
B3-3), ported for the qualifying-income environment's grader.

Source       apps/servicing/src/domain/verification/{ops-22-3,ops-22-1}.ts
Vendored at  293261b371fe97d2b6b39e1f29ca60dd808ac8fb (apps/servicing/VENDORED_FROM)
Ported       2026-10-21

Each file here is its source byte for byte except its import lines, and the
rewrite is two rules and no more:
  - the kernel's paths (../../kernel/{calendar,money,events}/<file>.ts)
    become @hm/kernel/{calendar,money,events} (the same bytes;
    packages/kernel/VENDORED_FROM);
  - a sibling's ./<file>.ts becomes ./<file>.js.
Nothing else may be edited: src/__tests__/income.test.ts rewrites each
original's imports by those rules, compares the result and the rest of the
file, and fails on any difference. Lint and Prettier skip this directory for
the reason they skip the vendored trees.

ops-22-1.ts is 807 lines of document-intake code (classification, freshness,
the needs list) that comes only because ops-22-3.ts imports three constants
from it: AGENT, CREDITOR_TZ and RULE_SET_VERSION. Nothing graded calls it.

The grader calls only the pure calculators in ops-22-3.ts (baseSalary,
hourlyBase, variableIncome, socialSecurity, supportIncome,
retirementDistribution, publicAssistance, subjectRental, regbCheck,
continuance3y, totalQualifying). It never calls calculateIncome,
evaluateContinuance, finalizeIncome or any VVOE, 4506-C or DU function:
they append events to an EventStore, and several mint ids from the
module's `ids` counter. The conventions around the calculators (the
dispatch by type and its defaults, the Reg B check, used_for_qualifying,
the continuance gate, the total) live in the service's stateful
app/tools/section22-3.ts and calculateIncome; they are restated in
src/income/grader.ts with the lines they come from.

To take a change from the servicing app, copy the two files again and apply
the same two rewrites.
